**W2-D3-Encapsulation_Private_Variables**

---

**What and Why:**
Encapsulation means hiding internal data so it can't be changed carelessly from outside. Without encapsulation anyone can write `acc.balance = -99999` directly and break your data silently — no error, just wrong data in database. This is why Django models use validation and why direct database edits are considered bad practice. Real backend APIs always control data access through methods, never expose raw data directly.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: PRIVATE VARIABLES — DOUBLE UNDERSCORE
# ============================================================

class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, owner, balance, pin):
        self.owner = owner
        # public attribute — accessible from anywhere
        # acc.owner = "Ravi" works from outside — that's fine

        self.__balance = balance
        # PRIVATE attribute — double underscore prefix
        # acc.__balance from outside = AttributeError — blocked
        # only accessible inside this class through methods
        # forces every change to go through validation

        self.__pin = pin
        # private — nobody can read or change pin directly from outside
        # must use change_pin() method which validates old pin first

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount <= 0:
            print("Invalid amount")
            return
        self.__balance += amount
        # accessing private attribute INSIDE class — allowed
        # only inside the class can you use self.__balance
        print(f"Deposited {amount}. New balance: {self.__balance}")

    def withdraw(self, amount):
        if amount <= 0:
            print("Invalid amount")
            return
        if amount > self.__balance:
            print("Insufficient funds")
            return
        self.__balance -= amount
        print(f"Withdrew {amount}. New balance: {self.__balance}")

    def get_balance(self):
        # GETTER METHOD — controlled way to READ private data
        # outside code calls this to see balance
        # can add logic here — e.g. log every balance check
        return self.__balance

    def set_balance(self, amount):
        # SETTER METHOD — controlled way to CHANGE private data
        # validates before allowing change
        if amount >= 0:
            self.__balance = amount
        else:
            print("Balance cannot be negative")
            # blocked — private data protected

    def change_pin(self, old_pin, new_pin):
        # validation — must know old pin to change it
        if old_pin == self.__pin:
            self.__pin = new_pin
            print("PIN changed successfully")
        else:
            print("Wrong PIN")
            # old pin doesn't match — change blocked


# ============================================================
# TESTING
# ============================================================

acc1 = BankAccount("Ravi", 1000, 1234)

acc1.deposit(500)
# Deposited 500. New balance: 1500

print(acc1.get_balance())
# 1500 — works through getter

acc1.set_balance(-500)
# Balance cannot be negative — blocked

acc1.set_balance(2000)
# allowed — positive value
print(acc1.get_balance())
# 2000

acc1.change_pin(1234, 5678)
# PIN changed successfully

acc1.change_pin(1234, 9999)
# Wrong PIN — blocked, old pin already changed
```

